#
# CI overlay: GCC static analyzer (-fanalyzer). Applied AFTER
# sdkconfig.defaults(.esp32s3):
#   idf.py -D SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.ci.analyzer" build
# BLOCKING in CI (since 2026-08-21), confined to this one configuration.
# Scope: -fanalyzer is applied to our components and to the ESP-IDF components
# the trimmed build pulls in (ADR 0017: set(COMPONENTS main) - no lwIP, Wi-Fi,
# BT). Registry (managed) components get -fno-analyzer from the top-level
# CMakeLists.txt because their findings are not ours to fix (esp-dsp 1.8.2:
# va_start without va_end, unchecked malloc; esp_lvgl_port 2.9.0: a leak and an
# uninitialised read on error paths - reported upstream). History: the first
# CI run (2026-08-21) failed on a -Wanalyzer-null-dereference inside lwIP's
# tcp_out.c before the component set was trimmed. Confirmed false positives in
# OUR code are suppressed inline with
# ESP_COMPILER_DIAGNOSTIC_PUSH_IGNORE("-Wanalyzer-...") from esp_compiler.h.
# clang-check (IDF_TOOLCHAIN=clang) stays advisory and is not a Kconfig overlay.
#
# Local multi-config builds MUST give each build dir its own sdkconfig, or the
# analyzer setting leaks into the default build through the shared ./sdkconfig:
#   idf.py -B build_an -D SDKCONFIG=build_an/sdkconfig \
#          -D SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.ci.analyzer" build

# `depends on IDF_TOOLCHAIN_GCC` (the default toolchain).
CONFIG_COMPILER_STATIC_ANALYZER=y
